# Time is everything: washout, new users and drug coverage in pandas
prompt: "What is a washout period, and why does it decide whether a patient counts as a new user? How do you turn dispensing dates and DDD into continuous periods of drug coverage? Why are incidence and prevalence two entirely different queries against the same dataset? What can merge_asof do that an ordinary merge cannot?"
next: {"title":"The numbers themselves: diabetes drug use and contacts in Norway","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/registerdata-i-praksis-python-og-pandas/tallene-selv-bruk-av-diabeteslegemidler.cast"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

## Time is everything — is this a new user?
lang: en
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,30],"y":[0,10]}

    text tittel "Time is everything — is this a new user?" x 500 y 706 font_size 34 hidden true
    axes ax x_label "Time (months from 2018-01-01)" hidden true
    dot p1 #2f6b8f at.x 4 at.y 5 hidden true
    label lab_p1 "2018-05-02" below #2f6b8f attach_to p1 hidden true
    dot p2 #b5482e at.x 14.4 at.y 5 hidden true
    label lab_p2 "2019-03-14" below #b5482e attach_to p2 hidden true
    dot p3 #2f6b8f at.x 17.6 at.y 5 hidden true
    text lab_p3 "Jun 19" above p3 gap 8 #2f6b8f font_size 22 hidden true
    dot p4 #2f6b8f at.x 21 at.y 5 hidden true
    text lab_p4 "Sep 19" below p4 gap 8 #2f6b8f font_size 22 hidden true
    dot p5 #2f6b8f at.x 24.2 at.y 5 hidden true
    text lab_p5 "Jan 20" above p5 gap 8 #2f6b8f font_size 22 hidden true
    arrow gap_arrow #8f887c from.x 4 from.y 3.2 to.x 14.4 to.y 3.2 hidden true
    label lab_gap "316 days without A10" below #8f887c attach_to gap_arrow hidden true
    arrow w12 #8a5fa8 from.x 14.4 from.y 7.2 to.x 2.4 to.y 7.2 hidden true
    text lab_w12 "12-month washout" #8a5fa8 at.data [8.4,7.85] font_size 24 hidden true
    arrow w6 #d0865f from.x 14.4 from.y 8.7 to.x 8.4 to.y 8.7 hidden true
    text lab_w6 "6-month washout" #d0865f at.data [11.4,9.35] font_size 24 hidden true
    arrow trunk dashed #8f887c from.x 4 from.y 1.6 to.x 0 to.y 1.6 hidden true
    label lab_trunk "only 121 days of history" below #8f887c attach_to trunk hidden true

The incidence in your study can double without a single row in lmr changing — this series is about why, and about how to write “new user” in pandas.
    draw tittel

    draw ax

Here is pid 4711: one dispensing of metformin, A10BA02, on the second of May 2018 — and our extract starts on the first of January that year.
    draw p1 lab_p1

    pause 1

Then it goes quiet for a long time. The next metformin dispensing comes on the fourteenth of March 2019.
    draw p2 lab_p2

    pause 1

From then on the dispensings come close together: June, September, January 2020 — a patient in steady treatment.
    draw p3 lab_p3 p4 lab_p4 p5 lab_p5 parallel true

    pause 1

So: should March 2019 count as a new user, or as someone resuming treatment?
    point at.ref p2 gesture circle

    wait

Between the first two dispensings there are 316 days without a single A10 dispensing.
    draw gap_arrow lab_gap

    pause 1

A washout period is the window you look back through from a dispensing; if it holds nothing from the same drug group, we call that dispensing a new user.
    draw w12 lab_w12

    pause 1

Twelve months reaches back to the fourteenth of March 2018 — and the May dispensing lies inside it.
    highlight w12 p1 effect glow

    pause 1

316 is less than 365, so under the twelve-month rule this is not a new user.
    highlight lab_gap effect pulse

    wait

Shrink the window to six months, and it starts on the fourteenth of September 2018.
    draw w6 lab_w6

    pause 1

Now the window is empty — same patient, same rows, opposite answer.
    highlight w6 effect glow

    pause 1

So the length of the washout is not a technical detail but an analytical decision, one you must justify and test in a sensitivity analysis.
    mark mark_p2 target lab_p2 kind box
    delivery grave

    pause 1

The idea comes from Wayne Ray's new-user design of 2003: prevalent users hide both the early side effects and the selection of people who already tolerate the drug.

And the very first dispensing cannot be classified at all — in front of it lie only 121 days of history.
    draw trunk lab_trunk

    pause 1

Left truncation is a hole in the observation window, not in the data, and that is why the cohort must always start later than the file does.
    highlight trunk effect glow

    wait

    clear

Four steps take us from this timeline to code that holds up on millions of rows.
    text road_title "The series in four steps" x 500 y 636 font_size 32

First, washout in pandas: sort by pid and date, compute the gap to the previous dispensing, and keep the ones with a big enough gap in front.
    text steg1 "1.  Washout in code: a gap before the dispensing" x 500 y 520 font_size 26
    mark mark_steg1 target steg1 kind box

    pause 1

Then from dispensing date and DDD to continuous coverage periods — how many days does one pack really last?
    text steg2 "2.  From DDD to continuous coverage" x 500 y 436 font_size 26

    pause 1

Then incidence versus prevalence: do you count new users, or all users in a window — two queries against the same lmr.
    text steg3 "3.  Incidence vs prevalence: two queries, one lmr" x 500 y 352 font_size 26

    pause 1

And finally merge_asof, which links each contact to the nearest dispensing before it — something an ordinary merge on pid can never do.
    text steg4 "4.  merge_asof: link to the nearest event before" x 500 y 268 font_size 26

    pause 1

Patient 4711 is a new user in March 2019 with a six-month washout, and is not one with twelve: the number you choose is part of your result.
    delivery grave

## Where next
end_page: true

